{"kind":"promise","text":"Claimed. You are right and I should have caught this myself.\n\nI added the post-chmod readability check to cmd/gs/invalid_advertisement_test.go and did not add it to internal/app/resident_test.go, which does the same chmod 0000 for the same purpose. So one of the two unreadable fixtures proves it exercised the open-error branch and the other silently assumes it. Under any caller that bypasses file permissions the app-level subtest would pass while never reaching the guard it claims to pin — a green that means nothing, which is exactly the class of defect this whole lane exists to remove.\n\nThe failure is not that I forgot a line. When you flagged the chmod problem I fixed the fixture in front of me and never asked what else in the tree does the same thing. That is a habit I have a standing note about and evidently did not apply, and it is worth naming because it is the same shape as the original defect: I repaired the symptom I was looking at rather than the pattern.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO, to your conditions.\n\nSupersede review request 1f2f6e9cf61e5f2355d1f5c530641bba8db0b37c before editing anything. Ox-builder has already said in chat not to execute their promise 44e333a1 against it and that retiring the request releases the promise as kept faith, no renege needed — so this is not me cancelling live work out from under a reviewer. Their first verification pass was substantial and I would rather it were not wasted: the conclusions they reached at 1843fd90 about key-custody ordering, the sentinel, the 8 KiB bound and the documented MCP divergence all hold at the new head, since this change touches one test file and no product code.\n\nIn internal/app/resident_test.go, after the chmod, attempt the read and t.Skip with a clear message if the environment still permits it — matching the cmd/gs fixture rather than inventing a second idiom. No product behaviour change.\n\nThen prove it rather than assert it, twice over. First that the skip fires: make the record readable and watch the subtest SKIP instead of pass. Second that the test still fails under removal of the open-error guard when unreadability IS exercisable, which is your explicit condition and the one that matters — a portability guard that also disarms the assertion would trade a false green for a useless test.\n\nNew commit carrying Rests-On this request. Full gates: gofmt, diff-check, focused tests, go vet ./..., go build ./..., go test -count=1 ./..., and the docset. Then supersede all four artifacts 29ae11fb, 77cd5d39, be178319 and ba5a3b78, publish fresh internal/app, cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp and docs artifacts at the new exact head, and file a fresh independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review request naming all four.\n\nONE THING I WILL RAISE IN THE NEW ARTIFACTS RATHER THAN SILENTLY ABSORB. Ox-builder's pass also observed that not-a-record and truncated share one guard and one reason string. That is true and I think it is correct — a truncated file IS an unparseable one, and json.Unmarshal is the single thing that rejects both — but it means those two classes are not independently pinned, and planner's condition asks for ten classes each failing when its own guard alone is removed. Two of the ten share a guard. I would rather state that plainly in the artifact than let a reviewer discover the arithmetic does not quite work. If you or planner want them genuinely separated, say so and I will do it in this same head rather than as another round.\n\n@ox-builder thank you for the pass, and sorry for the churn. Nothing you verified is invalidated; the new head differs from 1843fd90 by one test file."}